<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 7: Handling Factors                                      -->
<!--    Checked over and happy with it (for now at least!)             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-factors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Factors</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-factors">
    <p>
      In 1950, the South African government made a decision that would shape the
      lives of millions of people for decades: it decided to write down,
      officially and legally, what race everyone was.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this chapter, we are going to look carefully at variables like this
      one: variables that take a fixed set of named values, where the choice of
      what those values are, and how they are defined, has consequences for
      every analysis that follows.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: When a category changes the data                      -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-population-group-factors">
    <title>When a Category Changes the Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The story of "population group" is a story about how a single variable
        can shape an entire system of laws, and how that system can change over
        time.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-legal-classification">
      <title>A classification imposed by law</title>

      <p>
        In 1950, the South African government passed the Population Registration
        Act, which required every person living in South Africa to be officially
        classified into a racial category.
        At its simplest, the Act established three categories: White, Coloured,
        and Native (also referred to as <q>Bantu</q> ).
        Indians - descendants of South Asians who had come to South Africa under
        British rule - were later added as a fourth category, on the stated
        grounds that they had <q>no historical right to the country</q>.
      </p>

      <p>
        But the classification system did not stop there.
        Within each of these top-level categories, the law permitted and
        eventually required further subdivisions.
        By 1959, the Coloured category alone had been broken into seven
        subgroups: Cape Coloured, Cape Malay, Griqua, Chinese, Indian, Other
        Asiatic, and Other Coloured.
        The criteria used to assign people to these groups included physical
        appearance, how a person was generally regarded within their community,
        and their descent.
        In other words, the same person could in principle be classified
        differently depending on who was doing the classifying.
      </p>

      <p>
        This racial classification becamethe foundation on which a whole system
        of laws was built.
        The Group Areas Act determined where a person of each category was
        permitted to live.
        The Bantu Authorities Act restructured governance for those classified
        as Native, placing authority in the hands of traditional tribal leaders
        within designated homelands.
        The Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act made it illegal for a White
        person to marry someone of another classification.
        The Immorality Amendment Act went further, criminalising sexual
        relations between a white person and any individual of another
        classification.
        A person's most basic rights and opportunities, such as where they
        lived, who they could marry, what schools their children attended, and
        what work they could do, came from a single categorical variable
        assigned at birth.
      </p>

      <p>
        We begin the chapter on factors here because this is one of the starkest
        examples in history of what it means to reduce a human being to a
        category, and of the real consequences that categorical variables can
        carry.
      </p>

      <p>
        In statistics, we call this kind of variable a <term>factor</term> — a
        variable that takes one of a fixed set of values, each called a
        <term>level</term>.
        The Population Registration Act created a factor with a small number of
        levels, defined by law, and then built an entire system on top of it.
        In data analysis, the stakes are rarely so high — but the principle is
        the same.
        The levels you define, and how you define them, shape every summary,
        every comparison, and every model that follows.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Population Registration Act, 1950 (Act No.
        30 of 1950), and Population Registration Act Repeal Act, 1991 (Act No.
        114 of 1991), Parliament of South Africa.
        South Africa Population Census 1980, population-group variable codebook,
        World Bank Microdata Library.
        Khalfani, A.A.
        and Zuberi, T.
        (2001).
        <q>Racial
        classification and the modern census in South Africa,
        1911<ndash/>1996.</q>
        <em>Race and Society</em>, 4(2), 161 <ndash/> 176.
        Statistics South Africa (2023).
        <em>Census 2022: Statistical
        Release P0301.4.</em> Pretoria:
        Stats SA.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: ordered vs. unordered, and when to factor at all       -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-factors-ordered-vs-unordered">
    <title>TODO -- types of factors / illustrative example in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Factors come in two kinds.
        The first are <term>unordered factors</term>, where the levels are
        simply distinct categories with no natural ranking between them.
        The racial classifications of the Population Registration Act are one
        example: there is no sense in which "White" comes before or after
        "Coloured" in any meaningful order.
        Continent is another: "Africa", "Asia", "Europe", and "the Americas" are
        different, but not greater or lesser than one another.
        In data analysis, most categorical variables are unordered factors of
        this kind.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second kind are <term>ordered factors</term>, where the levels have
        a natural sequence that carries real meaning.
        The recent Competency Based Curriculum (CBC) in Kenya has very clear
        ordered levels: "Pre-Primary", "Lower Primary", "Upper Primary", "Junior
        Secondary", "Senior Secondary", "University".
        When researchers and policymakers analyse educational outcomes, the
        ordering matters.
        A student at Upper Primary is not just different from one at Junior
        Secondary, they are at a lower stage in a meaningful progression.
        Ordering within a factor can and should inform how we summarise and
        analyse the data.
      </p>

      <p>
        A starker example comes from humanitarian data.
        The Integrated Phase Classification (IPC) system, used across Kenya and
        the wider East Africa region, categorises food security into five
        ordered levels: "Minimal", "Stressed", "Crisis", "Emergency", and
        "Famine".
        This classification is used to help governments and international
        agencies track food shortages, malnutrition, and death rates to direct
        emergency aid.
        The ordering is not incidental: Crisis is meaningfully worse than
        Stressed, and Emergency is meaningfully worse than Crisis.
        If an analyst were to treat these five levels as an unordered factor,
        any summary or comparison they produced would be misleading at best, and
        at worst could inform decisions that cost lives.
      </p>

      <p>
        In R, the tools for working with factors are built into the base
        language, and extended by the <c>forcats</c> package (part of the
        tidyverse).
        Before working with a factor, it is worth knowing three basic checks:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      library(dplyr)
      library(forcats)
      library(readxl)
      library(lubridate)

      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")

      # Is VICTIM already a factor?
      is.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] FALSE

      # Convert it to one
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(VICTIM = as.factor(VICTIM))

      is.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] TRUE

      # What are its levels?
      # (Note that we are assuming this data is not cleaned yet,
      # so there are some typos and duplicates in the levels.
      # This can be another approach to data cleaning to look
      # at the levels to then fix them!)
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] "2 PASSENGERS &amp; DRIVER"       "2 PEDESTRIANS &amp; PASSENGER"  
      #&gt; [3] "4 PASSENGER 1 DRIVER"        "7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER"      
      #&gt; [5] "DRIVER"                      "DRIVER PASSENGER"           
      #&gt; [7] "M/CYCLIST"                   "M/CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"    
      #&gt; [9] "MOTOR CYCLIST"               "MOTOR CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"
      #&gt; [11] "MOTOR CYCLIST P/PASSENGER"   "P/CYCLIST"                  
      #&gt; [13] "P/PASENGER"                  "P/PASSENGER"                
      #&gt; [15] "PASSENGER"                   "PASSENGERS"                 
      #&gt; [17] "PEDAL CYCLIST"               "PEDESTRIAN"                 
      #&gt; [19] "RIDER"

      # Is it ordered?
      is.ordered(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] FALSE
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>VICTIM</c> is an unordered factor — there is no defensible ranking
        between pedestrians, drivers, and passengers.
        But now extract the month from the date column.
        Month has a clear, universally agreed order: January comes before
        February, February before March.
        We can create an ordered factor from it:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(
          month = lubridate::month(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`, label = TRUE)
        )

      # What does this give us?
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] "Jan" "Feb" "Mar" "Apr" "May" "Jun" "Jul" "Aug" "Sep" "Oct" "Nov" "Dec"

      is.ordered(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] TRUE
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>lubridate::month(label = TRUE)</c> returns month as an ordered factor
        automatically.
        It knows that April comes before June.
        We can verify that the ordering is meaningful by comparing levels
        directly:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016$month[1] &lt; kenya_accidents_2016$month[2]
      #&gt; [1] FALSE

      # June comes after April, so April &lt; June is TRUE
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$month)[4] &lt; levels(kenya_accidents_2016$month)[6]
      #&gt; [1] TRUE
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        An ordered factor also can give some statistics that are not meaningful
        for an unordered factor.
        For example, the maximum or minimum month in a dataset is meaningful.
        For example, if we have a dataset with months January, February, and
        March, the minimum month is January, and the maximum month is March.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      min(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] Apr
      #&gt; 12 Levels: Jan &lt; Feb &lt; Mar &lt; Apr &lt; May &lt; Jun &lt; Jul &lt; Aug &lt; Sep &lt; Oct &lt; ... &lt; Dec

      max(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] Jun
      #&gt; 12 Levels: Jan &lt; Feb &lt; Mar &lt; Apr &lt; May &lt; Jun &lt; Jul &lt; Aug &lt; Sep &lt; Oct &lt; ... &lt; Dec
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        If we needed to change the order of an unordered factor's levels, for
        example, to control how they appear in a plot, <c>fct_relevel()</c> from
        the <c>forcats</c> package lets us specify the order explicitly:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Default level order for VICTIM is alphabetical
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] "DRIVER"      "M/CYCLIST"   "P/CYCLIST"   "PASSENGER"
      #&gt; [5] "PEDESTRIAN"  "P/PASSENGER"

      # Reorder to put PEDESTRIAN first
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(VICTIM = fct_relevel(VICTIM, "PEDESTRIAN", "PASSENGER",
                                    "DRIVER", "M/CYCLIST", 
                                    "P/PASSENGER", "P/CYCLIST"))

      # Check the new order
      # Note that the levels not mentioned in `fct_relevel()` are still there,
      # but now come after the ones we specified
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt;  [1] "PEDESTRIAN"                  "PASSENGER"                  
      #&gt;  [3] "DRIVER"                      "M/CYCLIST"                  
      #&gt;  [5] "P/PASSENGER"                 "P/CYCLIST"                  
      #&gt;  [7] "2 PASSENGERS &amp; DRIVER"       "2 PEDESTRIANS &amp; PASSENGER"  
      #&gt;  [9] "4 PASSENGER 1 DRIVER"        "7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER"      
      #&gt; [11] "DRIVER PASSENGER"            "M/CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"    
      #&gt; [13] "MOTOR CYCLIST"               "MOTOR CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"
      #&gt; [15] "MOTOR CYCLIST P/PASSENGER"   "P/PASENGER"                 
      #&gt; [17] "PASSENGERS"                  "PEDAL CYCLIST"              
      #&gt; [19] "RIDER"
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> World Hunger Education Service.
        (2024, December).
        Famine fact sheet.
        https://www.worldhunger.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Famine-Fact-Sheet-Dec-2024.pdf
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-numeric-or-factor">
      <title>Numeric, unordered, or ordered: it is a judgement call</title>

      <p>
        Not every variable announces what it is.
        A column of numbers might represent a quantity you can meaningfully add
        and average, or it might be a label that happens to have been written as
        a number.
        A column of text might be a free-form description, or it might be one of
        a fixed set of categories.
        The data itself does not always tell you which; that is a judgement you
        have to make, and it has consequences for every analysis that follows.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>CAUSE CODE</c> column in the Kenya Road Accidents data is a good
        example.
        It contains numbers (7, 25, 98, 26, 63) and R reads it in as numeric by
        default.
        But these numbers are not quantities.
        They are administrative codes assigned to categories of crash cause.
        Code 7 means one thing; code 25 means another.
        There is no sense in which code 50 is halfway between code 25 and code
        75, or in which the average cause code of a set of crashes tells you
        anything useful.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # R reads CAUSE CODE as numeric
      class(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`)
      #&gt; [1] "numeric"

      # This lets R do arithmetic on it - but should it?
      mean(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`, na.rm = TRUE)
      #&gt; [1] 47.430...

      # The mean cause code is meaningless. Convert to factor instead:
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(`CAUSE CODE` = as.factor(`CAUSE CODE`))

      # Now R treats it as a set of labels
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`)
      #&gt;  [1] "1"  "4"  "6"  "7"  "8"  "9"  "10" "11" "14" "15" "16" "17" "19" "20" "21" "22"
      #&gt; [17] "25" "26" "28" "29" "30" "35" "36" "38" "39" "42" "44" "47" "50" "52" "54" "57"
      #&gt; [33] "58" "59" "60" "61" "63" "64" "66" "68" "69" "70" "71" "78" "79" "80" "81" "86"
      #&gt; [49] "92" "98"

      # And refuses to average them
      mean(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`, na.rm = TRUE)
      #&gt; Warning in mean.default(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`, na.rm = TRUE):
      #&gt;   argument is not numeric or logical: returning NA
      #&gt; [1] NA
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        That warning is R correctly refusing to do something meaningless.
        The decision to convert <c>CAUSE CODE</c> from numeric to factor is not
        a formatting choice.
        It is a claim about what the variable represents.
      </p>

      <p>
        The decision comes down to what you are claiming is true about the
        variable.
        Is the difference between one value and the next a meaningful quantity;
        something you could add, subtract, or average?
        Then store it as a number.
        Are the values simply distinct labels, with no numeric relationship
        between them?
        Then store it as an unordered factor.
        Do the values have a natural order that carries real information, but
        where the gaps between levels are not meaningful quantities?
        Then store it as an ordered factor.
        Getting this wrong can produce results that are arithmetically valid but
        analytically meaningless.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-recoding">
      <title>When the categories change: Recoding factor levels</title>

      <p>
        Defining the levels of a factor is rarely a one-time decision.
        In practice, the categories you start with are often not quite the
        categories you need for your analysis.
        Some levels may be too fine-grained, with so few observations in each
        that no meaningful comparison is possible.
        Others may reflect distinctions that matter administratively but not
        analytically.
        And sometimes a classification scheme has simply changed over time, and
        you need to align old labels with new ones.
      </p>

      <p>
        This last problem is one that analysts working with South African census
        data face directly.
        Under apartheid, the Population Registration Act imposed the racial
        classifications we described at the start of this chapter.
        After the transition to democracy, South Africa continued to collect
        data on race to measure the legacy of it and track progress towards
        equity.
        The 1996 census, the first held after apartheid, asked respondents to
        classify themselves using four categories: Black African, Coloured,
        Indian or Asian, and White.
        Subsequent censuses in 2001 and 2011 used the same four categories, but
        with slightly different wording and ordering across datasets and survey
        instruments.
      </p>

      <p>
        An analyst wanting to compare racial inequality across the 1996, 2001,
        and 2011 censuses cannot simply join the three datasets and treat the
        race variable as if it were identical across all three.
        The labels need to be checked, aligned, and in some cases collapsed or
        recoded before any comparison is meaningful.
        This is the work of recoding: adjusting the levels of a factor to better
        match the question you are actually trying to answer.
        It is about being deliberate and transparent about what distinctions you
        are treating as meaningful, and ensuring that the same word means the
        same thing across every dataset you are combining.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can see a simpler version of this in the Kenya accidents data.
        In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we created an <c>AGE_GROUP</c>
        variable that collapsed the raw <c>AGE</c> column, which contained a mix
        of numeric ages and codes like <c>A</c> (adult) and <c>J</c> (junior),
        into two clean categories.
        That recoding used <c>case_when()</c>.
        Once we have the categories, we can make them an explicit factor with a
        defined set of levels:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(
          AGE_GROUP = case_when(
            AGE %in% c("A", "ADULT")           ~ "ADULT",
            AGE %in% c("J", "JUNIOR")           ~ "JUNIOR",
            suppressWarnings(as.numeric(AGE)) &gt;= 18 ~ "ADULT",
            suppressWarnings(as.numeric(AGE)) &lt; 18  ~ "JUNIOR",
            TRUE ~ NA_character_
          ),
          # Make it an explicit unordered factor
          AGE_GROUP = factor(AGE_GROUP, levels = c("JUNIOR", "ADULT"))
        )

      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE_GROUP)
      #&gt; [1] "JUNIOR" "ADULT"

      table(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE_GROUP)
      #&gt; AGE_GROUP
      #&gt; JUNIOR  ADULT
      #&gt;    158    207 
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        By specifying <c>levels = c("JUNIOR", "ADULT")</c> explicitly, we
        control the order in which these categories appear in tables, plots, and
        model output.
        Without this, R would order them alphabetically, ( <c>ADULT</c> before
        <c>JUNIOR</c> ).
        With it, we get the more natural progression from younger to older.
      </p>

      <p>
        The IPC food security classification illustrates a different kind of
        recoding decision where the levels are collapsed or combined to match
        the question being asked.
        A researcher studying the difference between households in Crisis versus
        Emergency needs those levels kept separate.
        One asking simply <q>how many households are 
    food insecure?</q>
        might reasonably collapse Crisis, Emergency, and Famine into a single
        level.
        Neither choice is wrong, but both need to be made consciously and
        reported clearly.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-splitting-by-factors">
      <title>What a factor actually buys you: Splitting data by group</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          So far we have talked about what factors are and how to define them
          carefully.
          But why does it matter?
          What does treating a variable as a factor actually allow you to do?
        </p>

        <p>
          One answer is grouping.
          Once a variable is recognised as a factor, you can split your data on
          it, computing a separate summary for each level, or drawing a separate
          plot panel for each group, or fitting a separate model within each
          category.
          Every comparison you make between groups, every chart that shows one
          line per region or one bar per classification, every table that breaks
          results down by county or by IPC phase — all of it rests on treating
          the grouping variable as a factor and respecting its levels.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is a simple example using the Kenya accidents data.
          With <c>VICTIM</c> as a factor, we can count crashes by victim
          category in one line:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        group_by(VICTIM) %&gt;%
        summarise(crashes = n()) %&gt;%
        arrange(desc(crashes))
      #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 2
      #&gt;   VICTIM       crashes
      #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;          &lt;int&gt;
      #&gt; 1 PEDESTRIAN       152
      #&gt; 2 M/CYCLIST         82
      #&gt; 3 PASSENGER         50
      #&gt; 4 DRIVER            46
      #&gt; 5 P/PASSENGER       26
      #&gt; 6 P/CYCLIST          7
      #&gt; 7 MOTOR CYCLIST      2
      ...
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We can do the same by county, and by month to see whether crashes peak
          in certain months:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        group_by(month) %&gt;%
        summarise(crashes = n())
      #&gt; # A tibble: 3 × 2
      #&gt;   month crashes
      #&gt;   &lt;ord&gt;   &lt;int&gt;
      #&gt; 1 Apr       215
      #&gt; 2 May         0
      #&gt; 3 Jun       136
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Notice that <c>month</c> is displayed as <c>&lt;ord&gt;</c>, an
          ordered factor, and the months appear in calendar order, not
          alphabetical order.
          That ordering came from the factor definition, not from the data.
          May has zero crashes because, as we noted in
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, the dataset does not contain May
          data.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is also, looked at again, what actually happened in
          <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
          Berkeley's <c>department</c> was a factor in exactly this sense.
          The university-wide admission rate that looked like discrimination was
          computed by ignoring that factor, pooling every department into one
          group.
          Splitting by department meant grouping by the factor, and grouping by
          this factor was the single step that made the real pattern visible and
          reversed the conclusion.
          This is seen by facetting the plot in
          <xref ref="fig-berkeley-admissions-by-dept"/>.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-factors-grouping-payoff">
          <title>Grouping is what a factor is for</title>

          <p>
            A factor is not just a column that happens to hold category labels.
            It is the thing you can split your data <em>by</em>.
            Get a factor's levels wrong, as this chapter's opening story shows,
            and every group you compute is quietly the wrong group.
            Skip grouping by a factor that actually matters, as
            <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/> showed, and you get one number that
            describes no part of your data honestly.
            Treating a variable as a factor is not a formatting choice.
            It is a decision about which comparisons you are about to be allowed
            to make.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-example-flamingoes">
        <title>Example using Flamingoes Data</title>

        <p>
          The Kenya flamingo data gives us a cleaner illustration of what
          splitting by a factor makes possible.
          The dataset has 344 birds measured across three lakes (Bogoria,
          Elementaita, and Nakuru), two species (Greater Flamingo and Lesser
          Flamingo), and two sexes.
          Each of these factors is a natural factor to split on.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    library(ggplot2)
    library(dplyr)

    flamingos &lt;- read.csv("kenya_flamingos.csv") %&gt;%
      mutate(
        lake    = factor(lake),
        species = factor(species),
        sex     = factor(sex, levels = c("female", "male"))
      )

    # A simple split: mean body mass by species
    flamingos %&gt;%
      group_by(species) %&gt;%
      summarise(mean_mass = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))
    #&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 2
    #&gt;   species          mean_mass
    #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt; 1 Greater Flamingo     3120.
    #&gt; 2 Lesser Flamingo      1689.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Greater Flamingos are nearly twice the mass of Lesser Flamingos.
          But we have three factors we can split on — species, lake, and sex —
          and looking at them one at a time loses the interactions between them.
          We can build a plot that splits by all three at once.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    # Body mass by species and sex, faceted by lake
    flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(sex), sex != "") %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = sex, y = body_mass_g, fill = species)) +
        geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.7) +
        facet_wrap(~ lake) +
        scale_fill_manual(values = c("#F37021", "#44575E")) +
        labs(
          title = "Body mass of flamingos by sex, species, and lake",
          x = "Sex",
          y = "Body mass (g)",
          fill = "Species"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-mass-split">
          <caption>
            Body mass of Greater and Lesser Flamingos by sex, faceted by lake.
            Three factors (species, sex, and lake) are encoded simultaneously
            using position, fill colour, and facet panel.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_mass_split.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A set of three side-by-side boxplot panels, one per lake
                (Bogoria, Elementaita, Nakuru).
                Within each panel, body mass is shown on the y-axis with female
                and male on the x-axis.
                Two overlapping boxplots per x-axis position show Greater
                Flamingo (orange) and Lesser Flamingo (dark teal).
                Greater Flamingos are consistently heavier than Lesser Flamingos
                across all lakes and both sexes.
                Males tend to be slightly heavier than females within each
                species.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          We can also look at the relationship between bill length and body
          mass.
          Start with no factor split at all:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(bill_length_mm), !is.na(body_mass_g)) %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
        geom_point(alpha = 0.6, colour = "#44575E") +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE, colour = "#F37021") +
        labs(
          title = "Bill length vs body mass",
          x = "Bill length (mm)",
          y = "Body mass (g)"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-nosplit">
          <caption>
            Bill length against body mass for all 344 flamingos, with no species
            split.
            A single trend line is fitted across all birds.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_bill_mass_nosplit.png" width="80%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot with bill length in millimetres on the x-axis and
                body mass in grams on the y-axis.
                All points are the same dark teal colour.
                The points form two loose clusters — one in the lower left and
                one in the upper right — but without colour coding this
                structure is not immediately obvious.
                A single orange trend line runs through all points, suggesting a
                moderate positive relationship between bill length and body mass
                across all birds.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          There is a positive trend (longer bills, heavier birds), but the
          scatter is wide and the trend line is doing a lot of work over what
          looks like a somewhat awkward cloud of points.
          Something about this data does not quite fit a single straight line.
          Now add species as a factor, mapping it onto colour and shape:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(bill_length_mm), !is.na(body_mass_g)) %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g,
                 colour = species, shape = species)) +
        geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
        scale_colour_manual(values = c("#F37021", "#44575E")) +
        labs(
          title = "Bill length vs body mass by species",
          x = "Bill length (mm)",
          y = "Body mass (g)",
          colour = "Species",
          shape  = "Species"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-split">
          <caption>
            The same data as <xref ref="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-nosplit"/>, now
            split by species.
            What looked like a single awkward cloud resolves into two tightly
            clustered groups, each with its own clear relationship between bill
            length and body mass.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_bill_mass_split.png" width="80%">
            <description>
              <p>
                The same scatter plot as before, but points are now coloured
                orange for Greater Flamingo and dark teal for Lesser Flamingo,
                with different point shapes for each species.
                The two clusters are now clearly visible and entirely separate:
                Greater Flamingos in the upper right with longer bills and
                heavier bodies, Lesser Flamingos in the lower left.
                Each species has its own trend line, both showing tight positive
                relationships within the species that were obscured when all
                birds were plotted together.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The only thing that changed between the two plots was that we treated
          <c>species</c> as a factor and used it to split the plot in one of
          them.
          What looked like one moderate trend across a wide cloud of points
          resolves into two tighter clusters — each with a clearer relationship
          between bill length and body mass within the species.
          The overall trend was largely being driven by the difference
          <em>between</em> the two species, not by the relationship
          <em>within</em> either of them.
          Without the factor split, you would not know which of those two things
          you were looking at.
          This is exactly the kind of structural question that
          <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/> introduced: ignoring a grouping
          variable does not make it go away; it just hides it inside your
          summary statistic.
        </p>

        <p>
          These summaries and plots required that <c>species</c>, <c>lake</c>,
          and <c>sex</c> were treated as factors so that <c>ggplot2</c> could
          split the data on them cleanly.
          The factor is the boundary; the plot is what you get to see once you
          respect it.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can push further and add sex to see whether the relationship
          between bill length and body mass differs between male and female
          birds within each species:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(bill_length_mm), !is.na(body_mass_g),
             sex %in% c("female", "male")) %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g,
                 colour = sex, shape = species)) +
        geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
        scale_colour_manual(values = c("female" = "#F37021",
                                       "male"   = "#44575E")) +
        labs(
          title = "Bill length vs body mass by species and sex",
          x     = "Bill length (mm)",
          y     = "Body mass (g)",
          colour = "Sex",
          shape  = "Species"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-sex">
          <caption>
            Bill length against body mass for flamingos, split by both species
            (point shape) and sex (colour).
            Orange points and lines are female; dark teal are male.
            Circles are Greater Flamingos; triangles are Lesser Flamingos.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_bill_mass_sex.png" width="90%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot with bill length in millimetres on the x-axis and
                body mass in grams on the y-axis.
                Points are coloured orange for female and dark teal for male,
                and shaped as circles for Greater Flamingo and triangles for
                Lesser Flamingo.
                Within Lesser Flamingos, males are slightly heavier than females
                across the bill length range, and both show a weak positive
                trend.
                Within Greater Flamingos, the male trend line slopes downward:
                birds with longer bills tend to be lighter, while the female
                trend is roughly flat.
                This reversal within Greater Flamingos is a surprising finding
                that would be invisible without the sex split.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Something unexpected appears.
          Within Lesser Flamingos, males are slightly heavier than females
          across the range of bill lengths, and both sexes show a weak positive
          trend (longer bills, slightly heavier birds).
          That is unremarkable.
          But within Greater Flamingos, the male trend line slopes
          <em>downward</em>: male Greater Flamingos with longer bills tend to be
          lighter, not heavier.
          The female trend is roughly flat.
          This reversal, where the direction of the relationship changes
          depending on which group you look at, is exactly what
          <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/> called Simpson's Paradox.
          The overall trend across all Greater Flamingos (from
          <xref ref="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-split"/> ) suggested a positive
          relationship.
          Split by sex, that trend reverses for males.
        </p>

        <p>
          Whether this reflects genuine biology (perhaps males with longer bills
          invest energy differently, or perhaps longer-billed males tend to be
          older and lighter) or whether it is an artefact of a small sample of
          Greater Flamingos, is a question for an ornithologist, not a
          statistician.
          What the factor split does is make the pattern visible in the first
          place.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
  </section>

