  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 4: Exercises              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-handling-factors">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-identify">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the following variables, say whether you would store it as
          a number, an unordered factor, or an ordered factor, and briefly
          explain why:
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The cause code in the Kenya road accidents data.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The IPC food security classification for a household.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The county in which a crash occurred.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The year a crash occurred.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The hour of the day a crash occurred.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-create-ordered">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the Kenya road accidents data, extract the hour of day from the
          <c>TIME_PARSED</c> column (created in
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> ) and store it as an ordered
          factor.
          Then use <c>group_by()</c> and <c>summarise()</c> to count the number
          of crashes per hour.
          Does the ordering of the factor change the output?
          What would happen if you used an unordered factor instead?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-recode-victim">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>VICTIM</c> column in the Kenya road accidents data contains
          entries like <c>M/CYCLIST</c>, <c>MOTOR CYCLIST</c>, and
          <c>MOTORCYCLIST</c> that all refer to the same category.
          Using <c>fct_recode()</c> or <c>case_when()</c>, collapse these into a
          single clean level.
          Then produce a bar chart of victim category counts.
          How does the chart change compared to using the uncleaned factor?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-ipc-collapse">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The IPC food security classification has five levels: Minimal,
          Stressed, Crisis, Emergency, and Famine.
          A researcher wants to report simply what proportion of households are
          food secure (Minimal or Stressed) versus food insecure (Crisis,
          Emergency, or Famine).
          Using <c>fct_collapse()</c> from the <c>forcats</c> package, create a
          new two-level factor from the five-level IPC variable.
          What information is lost by making this collapse, and when might it be
          justified?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-south-africa">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-factors-recoding"/> describes the challenge of comparing
          South African census data across 1996, 2001, and 2011, where the race
          variable used slightly different wording across surveys.
          Suppose the 1996 census used the level <c>"Indian or Asian"</c> and
          the 2001 census split this into <c>"Indian"</c> and <c>"Asian"</c> as
          separate levels.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What would happen if you appended the two datasets without
              recoding first?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code using <c>fct_recode()</c> or <c>case_when()</c> to
              standardise both datasets to use a single consistent set of levels
              before appending.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What assumption are you making when you collapse <c>"Indian"</c>
              and <c>"Asian"</c> back into <c>"Indian or Asian"</c>?
              Is that assumption defensible?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>